        "id": 0,
        "original_id": "649f3445-d8fd-5e50-9684-17540e07118e",
        "text": "This chunk is about:\n- People: Jocelyn Bell Burnell, Antony Hewish, Martin Ryle, John Arbuthnott, Lord Wilson of Tillyorn\n- Organizations: Royal Astronomical Society, Royal Society of Edinburgh\n- Roles: astrophysicist, postgraduate student, thesis supervisor, president\n- Concepts: radio pulsars, Nobel Prize in Physics, Eddington Medal\n\nFacts:\n- In 1969, Antony Hewish was awarded the Eddington Medal of the Royal Astronomical Society.\n- Susan Jocelyn Bell Burnell was born on 15 July 1943 and is a Northern Irish astrophysicist.\n- As a postgraduate student, Jocelyn Bell Burnell discovered the first radio pulsars while studying under her thesis supervisor Antony Hewish.\n- Antony Hewish shared the Nobel Prize in Physics with astronomer Martin Ryle for work connected to the discovery of radio pulsars.\n- In October 2011, John Arbuthnott succeeded Lord Wilson of Tillyorn as President of the Royal Society of Edinburgh.\n- In October 2014, Dame Jocelyn Bell Burnell succeeded John Arbuthnott as President of the Royal Society of Edinburgh.",
        "type": "TextSummary"
      },
      {
